    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter May 21 with a thin slate, forcing us to lean heavily on our high-efficiency assets. **Kazuma Okamoto** is our strongest play today, boasting a 65.31 score against Carlos Rod\u00f3n. Despite the lineup being pending, the projected #4 spot paired with the platoon advantage against a southpaw pushes his value well into the \"Strong\" start territory. **Dillon Dingler** follows closely with a 60.31 rating; his matchup against Cantillo is the most favorable of the day, as the dynamic platoon advantage and a premium #3 slot in the order provide a stable floor for our catcher position. **Carson Benge** rounds out the active roster at a 57.23, which qualifies as a marginal start; however, his elite sprint speed remains our primary catalyst for stolen base production, and his projected leadoff role provides enough volume to justify the start despite the neutral matchup against Cade Cavalli.\n\nGiven that heavy hitters like **Yordan Alvarez**, **Junior Caminero**, **Pete Crow-Armstrong**, and **Trea Turner** are sidelined due to team off-days, our bench management is simplified, though the lack of depth tests our efficiency. Because the Zebras rely on speed to overcome scarcity, the absence of **Pete Crow-Armstrong** is particularly painful today, as he would have been an auto-start for his stolen base potential alone. With **Kyle Schwarber**, **Christian Yelich**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Hyeseong Kim**, **Colt Keith**, **Zac Veen**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Gary Sanchez**, **Luke Raley**, **Josh Smith**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Pedro Ramirez**, and **Luis Pena** also inactive or off-roster for this specific sequence, we must rely on the calculated platoon boosts for **Dillon Dingler** and **Kazuma Okamoto** to carry our scoring categories. Keep a close watch on official lineup cards; should **Kazuma Okamoto** or **Carson Benge** slide down their respective orders, their marginal efficiency scores could dip, necessitating a shift toward conservative cap management.",
